sketchBlocks.bodyMarkdown:
CSS **inheritance** lets some properties flow from a parent element to its descendants. Text properties such as `color` and `font-family` commonly inherit.

```css
body {
  color: #1e293b;
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}
```

Paragraphs and headings inside `body` can use those values without repeating them in every rule, unless a more specific rule gives them another value.

Not every property inherits. Treat inheritance as a useful default mechanism, then confirm the behavior of each property as you learn it.

**Line by line:** `body` selects the page body and establishes a base text style. The opening `{` starts that rule. A text declaration inside it supplies a value nested content can use when it does not set its own value. The closing `}` ends the body rule. A later rule such as `.intro { ... }` targets a narrower set of elements and can provide a competing value. Reading the selectors, declaration values, and rule positions separately makes it easier to predict which style the browser computes.

When multiple rules target the same element and the same property, **specificity** helps determine which declaration wins.

For example:

```css
p {
  color: #475569;
}

.intro {
  color: #0f766e;
}
```

The introductory paragraph matches both selectors, but the class selector `.intro` is more specific than the type selector `p`, so its color wins.

Specificity is not a reason to make every selector long. Prefer the simplest selector that expresses the styling role clearly.

If two declarations have equal importance and equal specificity, the one that appears **later** in the stylesheet wins for the same property.

```css
h2 {
  color: slateblue;
}

h2 {
  color: darkgreen;
}
```

The second color wins because both selectors have the same specificity and target the same property.

This does not mean you should intentionally duplicate rules everywhere. Source order is part of the cascade, but a maintainable stylesheet should minimize accidental conflicts. When debugging, first ask: which rules match, which is more specific, and if specificity ties, which comes later?

